== Configuration ==

=== Configuration file ===

The <code>--config</code> file is JSON. Example (<code>config_example.json</code>):

<pre>
{
    "name": "CP_001",
    "database": "/opt/charger/data/chargepoint1.db",
    "port": 5000,
    "logs": {
        "ocpp_log": "/var/log/charger/stationocpp.log",
        "access_log": "/var/log/charger/access.log",
        "error_log": "/var/log/charger/error.log"
    }
}
</pre>

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Description
|-
| <code>name</code> || Charge point / station identifier used in OCPP and shown in the UI
|-
| <code>database</code> || Path to the SQLite database file
|-
| <code>port</code> || Port for the Flask web GUI (HTTP)
|-
| <code>logs.ocpp_log</code> || Path to the OCPP wire protocol log
|-
| <code>logs.access_log</code> || Path to the access/general log
|-
| <code>logs.error_log</code> || Path to the error log
|}

=== Runtime settings ===

The following settings are managed at runtime from the '''Settings''' dialog in the web UI (or the <code>/api/config</code> endpoint) and persisted in the database:

{| class="wikitable"
! Setting !! Default !! Meaning
|-
| <code>charge_point_vendor</code> || <code>DemoVendor</code> || Vendor name sent in BootNotification
|-
| <code>charge_point_model</code> || <code>PythonEmu</code> || Model name sent in BootNotification
|-
| <code>ocpp_url</code> || <code>ws://localhost:9000/ocpp</code> || WebSocket URL of the CSMS (reconnects on change)
|-
| <code>num_connectors</code> || <code>1</code> || Number of charging connectors
|-
| <code>max_kwh</code> || <code>50.0</code> || Maximum simulated power per session (kW)
|-
| <code>min_kwh</code> || <code>0.0</code> || Minimum simulated power per session (kW)
|-
| <code>id_tags</code> || <code>["DEFAULT_TAG"]</code> || RFID/ID tags sent in StartTransaction
|}

{{Note|The charge point connects to the CSMS at <code>&lt;ocpp_url&gt;/&lt;station_id&gt;</code>. Point this at the CSMS directly, or at the OCPPProxy (default <code>ws://&lt;proxy&gt;:9100/ocpp</code>).}}

=== Database ===

SQLite database (default <code>/opt/charger/data/chargepoint.db</code>), created automatically.

{| class="wikitable"
! Table !! Purpose
|-
| <code>config</code> || Key‑value runtime configuration (e.g. <code>ocpp_url</code>, <code>max_kwh</code>, <code>id_tags</code>)
|-
| <code>sessions</code> || Charging sessions (start/end time, energy, OCPP transaction id)
|-
| <code>ocpp_logs</code> || OCPP wire log entries (timestamp, severity, message)
|}

== Using the Web Interface ==

Open the GUI at <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:5000</code>. State updates are pushed live over Socket.IO.

=== Station Control (<code>/</code>) ===

The main page simulates physical events and charging:

* '''Cable''' — select a connector and connect/disconnect the charging cable.
* '''Car''' — simulate a vehicle being present or absent.
* '''Charging''' — start or stop a session. A session can only start when both the cable and car are connected.
* '''Energy indicators''' — cumulative total energy and current‑session energy.
* '''Charts''' — energy per session (bar) and cumulative energy over time (line), updating live.

=== Statistics (<code>/stats</code>) ===

Historical view of sessions: total session count, total energy, and a current‑charging indicator, plus a table of all sessions (ID, started, ended, duration, energy, status) sorted newest first. Active sessions show live‑updating duration and energy.

=== OCPP Log (<code>/ocpp</code>) ===

A searchable, auto‑refreshing table of OCPP messages and events with timestamp, severity (DEBUG/INFO/WARNING/ERROR), and message text. Use the search box to filter; the newest entries appear first.

=== Settings dialog ===

Edit the runtime settings (vendor, model, OCPP URL, number of connectors, ID tags, max/min kW). Saving persists the values immediately; changing the OCPP URL reconnects the charge point.

== REST API Reference ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Endpoint !! Method !! Purpose
|-
| <code>/api/state</code> || GET || Current charger state (cable/car/charging, energy, sessions, history)
|-
| <code>/api/cable</code> || POST || Connect/disconnect the cable: <code>{"action":"connect","connector_id":1}</code> or <code>{"action":"disconnect"}</code>
|-
| <code>/api/car</code> || POST || Connect/disconnect the car: <code>{"action":"connect"}</code> / <code>{"action":"disconnect"}</code>
|-
| <code>/api/charging</code> || POST || Start/stop charging: <code>{"action":"start"}</code> / <code>{"action":"stop"}</code>
|-
| <code>/api/config</code> || GET / POST || Get or update runtime configuration
|-
| <code>/api/register</code> || POST || Reboot — re‑send BootNotification to the CSMS
|-
| <code>/api/remote-start</code> || POST || Start charging (same as the UI button)
|-
| <code>/api/remote-stop</code> || POST || Stop charging
|-
| <code>/api/soft-reset</code> || POST || Soft reset (stop charging, keep state)
|-
| <code>/api/hard-reset</code> || POST || Hard reset (clear all state)
|-
| <code>/api/unlock-connector</code> || POST || Unlock a connector: <code>{"connector_id":1}</code>
|-
| <code>/api/ocpp-logs</code> || GET || Fetch OCPP logs (search via <code>?q=</code>)
|}

== The Simulator ==

The OCPP client (<code>cp_sim.py</code>) runs in a background asyncio thread started by <code>chargepoint.py</code>. It maintains the WebSocket connection to the CSMS, performs the BootNotification handshake, sends heartbeats and meter values, and handles remote commands.

=== Energy simulation ===

While a session is active, a background loop updates the simulated values periodically:

* '''Power''' — a value between <code>min_kwh</code> and <code>max_kwh</code>.
* '''Per‑phase current''' — derived from power (≈ power ÷ 230 V ÷ 3) with small random variation, reported as L1/L2/L3.
* '''Energy''' — power integrated over time, accumulated in kWh.

=== Sessions and connectors ===

Each session records its start time, energy, and the OCPP transaction id assigned by the CSMS, and is persisted to SQLite. The simulator supports 1–N connectors (<code>num_connectors</code>), with one active connector at a time. Connectors can be marked operative/inoperative via OCPP <code>ChangeAvailability</code>.

=== ID tags ===

The configured <code>id_tags</code> are sent in StartTransaction to simulate an RFID swipe (no real validation is performed).

== OCPP 1.6 Support Reference ==

=== Messages sent (Charge Point → CSMS) ===

{| class="wikitable"
! Message !! When !! Purpose
|-
| <code>BootNotification</code> || On startup and reconnect || Register with the CSMS (vendor/model); honours the returned retry interval
|-
| <code>Heartbeat</code> || Periodically || Keep‑alive
|-
| <code>StatusNotification</code> || On state change || Report connector status (Available, Preparing, Charging, Finishing, etc.)
|-
| <code>StartTransaction</code> || On session start || Begin a session; the CSMS returns a transaction id
|-
| <code>MeterValues</code> || While charging || Report energy (Wh), power (kW), and per‑phase current (A)
|-
| <code>StopTransaction</code> || On session end || End the session and report final energy and reason
|}

=== Commands handled (CSMS → Charge Point) ===

{| class="wikitable"
! Message !! Effect
|-
| <code>RemoteStartTransaction</code> || Start charging (requires cable and car connected)
|-
| <code>RemoteStopTransaction</code> || Stop the current session
|-
| <code>Reset</code> || Hard = clear all state; Soft = stop charging only
|-
| <code>UnlockConnector</code> || Simulate unlocking the connector
|-
| <code>ChangeAvailability</code> || Mark a connector operative/inoperative
|}

=== Reported connector statuses ===

<code>Available</code>, <code>Preparing</code>, <code>Charging</code>, <code>Finishing</code>, <code>Suspended_EV</code>, <code>Unavailable</code>.

=== Status logic ===

<pre>
Cable disconnected            → Available
Cable connected, no car       → Preparing
Car connected, not charging   → Suspended_EV
Actively charging             → Charging
Session finishing (soft stop) → Finishing
Connector inoperative         → Unavailable
</pre>

== Troubleshooting ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Symptom !! Things to check
|-
| Repeated reconnect attempts || Ensure the CSMS (or OCPPProxy) is reachable at <code>ocpp_url</code>; check the OCPP log.
|-
| Web UI not reachable || Verify the service is running and the configured <code>port</code> (5000) is open.
|-
| Cannot start a session || Both the cable and the car must be connected first.
|-
| Charging not authorised || Confirm the CSMS accepts the configured <code>id_tags</code>.
|-
| Logs growing large || Configure external log rotation (e.g. <code>logrotate</code>) for the JSON logs.
|-
| Service won't start || Inspect <code>/var/log/charger/chargepoint.stderr</code> and <code>journalctl -u charger-chargepoint</code>; confirm dependencies and the config path.
|}

== Running Multiple Charge Points ==

Run several simulators on one host by giving each its own config file with a distinct <code>name</code>, <code>port</code>, and <code>database</code> (and, if desired, separate log paths). Start each with its own <code>--config</code> argument or its own systemd unit.

== Quick Deployment Checklist ==

# Install Python 3 and run <code>pip install flask flask-socketio ocpp websockets</code>.
# Create <code>/opt/charger/ChargePoint/</code>, <code>/opt/charger/data/</code>, and <code>/var/log/charger/</code>.
# Add a config file (e.g. <code>config.json</code>) with the station <code>name</code>, <code>port</code>, and <code>database</code>.
# Install and enable the systemd service.
# Open the web UI at <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:5000</code>.
# In '''Settings''', set the <code>ocpp_url</code> to your CSMS (or OCPPProxy) and the number of connectors.
# Use the Station Control page to connect the cable and car, then start a session.
